{"title":"Common Question Types","markdown":{"headingText":"Common Question Types","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n**Descriptive questions:**\n- Use bullet points for clarity\n- One mark = one point\n- Use correct scientific terminology\n- Be concise but complete\n- Include relevant details\n\n**Graph questions:**\n- Use a ruler for straight lines\n- Label axes with quantities and units\n- Choose appropriate scales\n- Plot points accurately (±½ small square)\n- Draw smooth best-fit line\n- Calculate gradient using large triangle\n\n---\n\n\n**Definitions:**\n- Learn key definitions precisely\n- Include units where relevant\n- Examples: speed, acceleration, pressure, power\n\n**Laws and principles:**\n- State exactly as learned\n- Examples: Newton's laws, conservation of energy, Ohm's law\n\n**Explanations:**\n- Use the structure: \"Because... therefore...\"\n- Link cause and effect\n- Use particle model where appropriate\n- Refer to relevant physics principles\n\n**Comparisons:**\n- Address both items being compared\n- Use comparative language: \"greater than,\" \"less than,\" \"similar to\"\n- Give specific differences/similarities\n\n---\n\n## Practical Papers (5 & 6)\n\n### Paper 5: Practical Test\n\n**What to expect:**\n- Hands-on experiments in laboratory\n- Usually 3–4 experiments\n- 1 hour 15 minutes\n- 40 marks\n\n**Skills tested:**\n- Setting up apparatus correctly\n- Taking accurate measurements\n- Recording data in tables\n- Plotting and analyzing graphs\n- Drawing conclusions\n- Evaluating methods\n\n**Tips:**\n1. Read all instructions before starting\n2. Set up apparatus carefully\n3. Take readings to appropriate precision\n4. Record units in table headings\n5. Plot graphs neatly and accurately\n6. Show all working for calculations\n7. Comment on sources of error\n8. Suggest realistic improvements\n\n---\n\n### Paper 6: Alternative to Practical\n\n**What to expect:**\n- Written questions about practical work\n- No laboratory work required\n- 1 hour\n- 40 marks\n\n**Question types:**\n- Drawing and labeling apparatus\n- Completing data tables\n- Plotting graphs from given data\n- Analyzing results\n- Identifying errors\n- Suggesting improvements\n\n**Preparation:**\n- Know common experimental setups\n- Understand how to use standard apparatus\n- Practice drawing circuit diagrams\n- Learn how to plot and interpret graphs\n- Understand sources of error\n- Know how to improve experiments\n\n---\n\n## Revision Strategies\n\n### Before the Exam\n\n**Content review:**\n- Create summary notes for each topic\n- Make formula sheets\n- Practice past papers\n- Review practical work\n- Identify weak areas and focus on them\n\n**Formula learning:**\n- Understand what each symbol means\n- Know the units\n- Practice rearranging equations\n- Learn which formulas are provided\n\n**Practical skills:**\n- Review all required practicals\n- Know the methods\n- Understand sources of error\n- Practice data analysis\n\n---\n\n### During Revision\n\n**Active learning:**\n- Do not just read—practice\n- Solve problems without looking at solutions\n- Explain concepts to others\n- Create mind maps\n- Use flashcards for definitions\n\n**Past papers:**\n- Do under timed conditions\n- Mark using mark scheme\n- Identify common question types\n- Learn from mistakes\n- Track progress\n\n---\n\n## Exam Day\n\n### Before the Exam\n\n- Get adequate sleep\n- Eat a good breakfast\n- Arrive early\n- Bring required equipment:\n  - Calculator (check batteries)\n  - Ruler (30 cm)\n  - Protractor\n  - Compasses\n  - Sharp pencils (HB and 2B)\n  - Eraser\n  - Pen (black or blue)\n\n---\n\n### During the Exam\n\n**Time allocation:**\n- Check total time available\n- Allocate time per section\n- Leave time to check answers\n- Do not get stuck on one question\n\n**Checking work:**\n- Verify calculations make sense\n- Check units are correct\n- Ensure answers are reasonable\n- Review multiple choice answers\n- Check all questions answered\n\n**Common mistakes to avoid:**\n- Forgetting units\n- Wrong number of significant figures\n- Misreading scales on graphs\n- Confusing mass and weight\n- Mixing up series and parallel circuits\n- Forgetting to convert units (cm to m, etc.)\n- Not showing working for calculations\n\n---\n\n## Specific Topic Tips\n\n### Mechanics\n- Draw free-body diagrams\n- Resolve vectors when needed\n- Check directions of forces\n- Use correct sign convention\n\n### Thermal Physics\n- Remember temperature in Kelvin for gas laws\n- Distinguish between heat and temperature\n- Know particle model explanations\n\n### Waves\n- Distinguish transverse and longitudinal\n- Remember $v = f\\lambda$\n- Know electromagnetic spectrum order\n- Practice ray diagrams\n\n### Electricity\n- Draw clear circuit diagrams\n- Remember series and parallel rules\n- Check current direction\n- Know component symbols\n\n### Magnetism\n- Use Fleming's rules correctly\n- Know field patterns\n- Remember motor and generator effects\n\n### Nuclear Physics\n- Balance nuclear equations\n- Know properties of α, β, γ\n- Understand half-life calculations\n- Know safety precautions\n\n---\n\n## Mark Scheme Insights\n\n### How marks are awarded\n\n**Method marks (M):**\n- Awarded for correct method\n- Even if final answer is wrong\n- Show all working\n\n**Accuracy marks (A):**\n- Awarded for correct answer\n- Dependent on method mark\n- Must have correct units\n\n**Independent marks (B):**\n- Awarded independently\n- Often for definitions or statements\n- Do not depend on other marks\n\n**Error carried forward (ECF):**\n- If you make an early error\n- You can still get marks for correct method\n- Using your incorrect value correctly\n\n---\n\n### Maximizing marks\n\n**Show working:**\n- Even if answer is wrong\n- Method marks available\n- Helps if you make calculation error\n- Essential for full credit\n\n**Use correct terminology:**\n- Use physics terms precisely\n- Avoid vague language\n- Be specific\n\n**Answer the question:**\n- Read carefully what is asked\n- Do not provide irrelevant information\n- Match answer to command word\n\n**Check your answer:**\n- Does it make physical sense?\n- Are units correct?\n- Is magnitude reasonable?\n- Have you answered all parts?\n\n---\n\n## Final Checklist\n\n### Before submitting:\n\n- [ ] All questions attempted\n- [ ] Units included where required\n- [ ] Working shown for calculations\n- [ ] Graphs properly labeled\n- [ ] Answers to appropriate significant figures\n- [ ] Multiple choice answers clearly marked\n- [ ] Diagrams clearly drawn and labeled\n- [ ] Name and candidate number on all pages\n- [ ] Checked for careless errors\n- [ ] Time remaining used for checking","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":8,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"png","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":true,"freeze":false,"echo":false,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"python":"C:/Users/User/AppData/Local/Programs/Python/Python312/python.exe","message":false,"dpi":300,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":false,"code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":true,"link-external-newwind